&lt;p&gt;Join Devesh Kapur and Arvind Subramanian for a discussion of their new book, &lt;em&gt;A Sixth of Humanity: Independent India&amp;#39;s Development Odyssey&lt;/em&gt;, with discussant Mark W. Frazier. The book offers a sweeping account of India&amp;#39;s development since independence, organized around the interplay of state, society, nation, and markets. Kapur and Subramanian argue that India&amp;#39;s path has been &amp;quot;precocious&amp;quot;: democracy arrived before broad-based prosperity, and services grew ahead of manufacturing. They make the case that too much was asked of the Indian state, too often and too soon. Drawing on extensive data and comparisons across Indian states, the book asks what India&amp;#39;s experience reveals about democracy and development, and what lies ahead for a country home to a sixth of humanity. &lt;em&gt;A Sixth of Humanity&lt;/em&gt; was named one of the Financial Times Best Books in Economics of 2025.&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;br /&gt;
SPEAKERS&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Devesh Kapur&lt;/strong&gt; is the Starr Foundation Professor of South Asian Studies at the Johns Hopkins University School of Advanced International Studies (SAIS). He previously directed the Center for the Advanced Study of India at the University of Pennsylvania.&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Arvind Subramanian&lt;/strong&gt; is a Senior Fellow at the Peterson Institute for International Economics. He served as Chief Economic Adviser to the Government of India from 2014 to 2018.&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;br /&gt;
DISCUSSANT&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Mark W. Frazier &lt;/strong&gt;is Professor of Politics and Co-Director of the India China Institute at The New School.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Twenty-five years ago, Christopher Beha gave up on God and became a committed atheist. The former editor of &lt;em&gt;Harper&amp;rsquo;s Magazine&lt;/em&gt; and an acclaimed writer, Beha&amp;rsquo;s intellectual journey took him through the two major atheist worldviews: scientific materialism and romantic idealism. In his new book, &lt;em&gt;Why I Am Not an Atheist&lt;/em&gt;, Beha argues that faith preserves the best of both traditions while offering a complete and coherent picture of reality. In this talk, he will discuss his life-long engagement with enduring human questions.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;On Monday, November 16, at 5 PM join the Morningside Institute and the Earl Hall Center for Religious Life as we welcome &lt;strong&gt;Christopher Beha&lt;/strong&gt; for this talk in Earl Hall. Copies of the book will be available for purchase.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.morningsideinstitute.org/cal/2026/11/16/why-i-am-not-an-atheist" target="_blank"&gt;Register here&lt;/a&gt;.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Euan Cameron in conversation with Prof. Christopher Gillett&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Cosmic Time and the Divine Plan&lt;/em&gt; draws on extensive research to explore the relationship between theological thought about the story of human existence, and the histories and historical chronologies of the world, in the pre-modern period. It argues that until the sixteenth-century Reformation, two ways of thinking and two genres of writing, technical chronology on one hand and theological meaning-making in history on the other, were generally practised separately from one another, even though the relationship between them was acknowledged.&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;br /&gt;
With the Protestant Reformation, histories of the world, including those inherited from previous centuries, were brought within the scope of the movement&amp;#39;s theological project. Historical narrative demonstrated the hand of God at work in the apparently random events of history, especially when prophets foretold future events. The interpretation of Scripture, especially the prophetic writings, was thus understood to provide essential underpinning for understanding the story of world history. Biblical exegesis and historical chronology were interwoven: biblical commentaries, especially of the Book of Daniel, were often explained through detailed time-lines.&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;br /&gt;
This confessional argument reflected how the Reformation saw itself. The reformers understood the divine plan of salvation to be worked out over time, through rise and decline, exile and return, loss and recovery, in the life of the Church and in the life of the individual. This approach was distinctly Protestant: while Catholic scholars also carried out meticulous historical research, their historical vision presupposed a continuous and stable, rather than interrupted, relationship between the Church and the divine.&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;br /&gt;
In the seventeenth century, scholars pursued the goal of chronological exactitude with ever greater precision and skill in ancient calendars, without ever agreeing. The religious and theological rationale for the quest was never forgotten. Some calculators emphasized the use of astronomical data to refine calculations, culminating in Isaac Newton in the 1720s; others insisted that Scriptural evidence was more reliable. Only with changing intellectual fashions in the mid-eighteenth century did the quest for meaningful world history through Scriptural computations gradually forfeit the attention of the learned.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Registration REQUIRED by 4pm on November 16, 2026 in order to attend this event.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Please join the Harriman Institute for a book talk by&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;Miriam Dobson&lt;/strong&gt;. Moderated by&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;Yana Skorobogatov&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;The Unorthodox&lt;/em&gt; offers a new history of Ukraine and Russia&amp;rsquo;s Protestant communities, from the religious resurgence of World War II to the early 1980s. In this talk, Dobson focuses on the life of one Protestant woman, Lidiia Vins, to explore three of the book&amp;rsquo;s themes: gender relations within Baptist congregations; the place of Protestant writings within the history of Soviet dissent; and their reception overseas during the late Cold War.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Join the Columbia Law School Human Rights Institute for a conversation with Steve Crawshaw, author of the new book, &amp;ldquo;Prosecuting the Powerful&amp;rdquo;. At a time of ongoing armed conflicts and growing challenges to accountability for atrocity crimes, Crawshaw will discuss the pursuit of international justice and what it takes to hold powerful actors accountable for the world&amp;#39;s gravest crimes. The conversation will explore themes from the book alongside current developments in international criminal justice, including efforts toward a future Convention on the Prevention and Punishment of Crimes Against Humanity.&lt;/p&gt;
