&lt;p&gt;Fresh perspectives on five iconic Italian sites: Vincenzo Castella captures the shimmer of a church dome rising from the lagoon of Venice, the delicate but powerful arches of a monastery, and much more.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;These portraits of religious structures are as clear and direct as a Dutch painting, because Castella uses natural light, large-format negatives, and a meticulous printing process. He deliberately avoids the convention of immortalizing the &amp;ldquo;masterpiece&amp;rdquo; locale and focuses instead on evocative features. The exhibition alternates panoramic views with architectural details to build a meditative journey that is anchored in reality.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Locations:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
San Giorgio Maggiore church, Venice&lt;br /&gt;
Santa Maria delle Grazie church, Milan&lt;br /&gt;
Santa Maria del Fiore cathedral, Florence&lt;br /&gt;
Certosa di Pavia monastery, Pavia&lt;br /&gt;
Sant&amp;rsquo;Andrea basilica, Mantua&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Register &lt;a href="https://italianacademy.columbia.edu/events/renaissance-natural-light-photographs-vincenzo-castella" target="_blank"&gt;here&lt;/a&gt;.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;This registration is for the opening reception: Nov. 17, 6 PM &amp;ndash; 7 PM.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;After that day, the exhibition will remain open and NO registration will be needed. Opening hours will be weekdays, 10 AM &amp;ndash; 4 PM, throughout the semester.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr /&gt;
&lt;p&gt;ABOUT THE PHOTOGRAPHER&lt;br /&gt;
Vincenzo Castella was born in Naples in 1952 and now lives in Milan. He was initially inspired by the work of American photographers such as Paul Strand and Walker Evans; in 1984 he took part in Luigi Ghirri&amp;rsquo;s project &amp;ldquo;Viaggio in Italia.&amp;rdquo;&lt;br /&gt;
In the late 1990s, Castella began doing aerial work and large-format color photography, anticipating a trend in contemporary photography that would later define an entire generation of artists. This is when he began creating his renowned aerial views of European cities, which explore themes of distance and dislocation in a non-narrative way.&lt;br /&gt;
Since 2006, Castella has been creating installations using large-format negatives. His works are featured in major exhibitions and collections in Italy and abroad: MAXXI (Museo Nazionale delle arti del XXI Secolo) in Rome, MUFOCO (Museo di Fotografia Contemporanea) in Cinisello Balsamo, the Sandretto Re Rebaudengo Foundation in Turin, the Tate Modern in London, the Fine Arts Museum in Houston, and at the Venice Biennale and at Basel&amp;rsquo;s Art Unlimited. In 2023, he had a one-man exhibition at the Triennale di Milano.&lt;/p&gt;
