&lt;p&gt;Please join the Harriman Institute for a talk by &lt;strong&gt;Sheila Fitzpatrick&lt;/strong&gt;. Moderated by&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://harriman.columbia.edu/person/elise-giuliano/"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Elise Giuliano&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;As a US-based Soviet historian, Fitzpatrick was in and out of the Soviet Union during perestroika and the early post-Soviet period, doing research in newly opened archives but also talking to people and generally keeping her eyes open. Moscow, Leningrad/St Petersburg, Sverdlovsk. Yekaterinburg, Novosibirsk, Samara, and Riga were the main ports of call. This talk is drawn from her forthcoming memoir &lt;em&gt;1989: a Perestroika Love Story&lt;/em&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

