&lt;h3&gt;&lt;strong&gt;About Columbia Global&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://global.columbia.edu/" target="_blank"&gt;Columbia Global&lt;/a&gt; brings together five major global initiatives from across the university to advance knowledge and foster global engagement. Those initiatives include Columbia Global Centers, Columbia World Projects, Committee on Global Thought, Institute for Ideas and Imagination, and Center for Undergraduate Global Engagement.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Columbia Global&amp;rsquo;s mission is to address complex global challenges through groundbreaking scholarly pursuits, leadership development, cutting-edge research, and projects that aim for social impact. Our long-term goal is to reimagine the university&amp;rsquo;s role in society as a nexus for learning and intellectual exploration and also as a catalyst for creativity and impact locally, regionally, and globally.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This panel discussion will examine the role of Sufism in shaping Islamic renewal (&lt;em&gt;tajdīd&lt;/em&gt;) across the modern Islamic world, from the late seventeenth to the early twentieth century. It will challenge the view of Sufism as either marginal, conservative, or simply &amp;ldquo;purified&amp;rdquo; and rationalized, and will highlight Sufi networks as active sites of intellectual, ethical, and spiritual transformation. The event will interrogate categories such as &amp;ldquo;neo-Sufism&amp;rdquo; and &amp;ldquo;Sufi reformism&amp;rdquo; and will explore their analytical value and historical complexity through a comparative and transregional perspective on the modern Islamic world.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Panelists:&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Gianfranco Bria&lt;/strong&gt; (Roma Tre University) &amp;mdash; &lt;em&gt;&amp;ldquo;Renewal before Reform? Tajdīd and Sufi Networks in Modern Islam&amp;rdquo;&lt;/em&gt; &lt;em&gt;(Chair and Convener)&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Marouen Jedoui&lt;/strong&gt; (Yale University) &amp;mdash; &lt;em&gt;&amp;ldquo;Renewal, Not Reform: The Karkariyya, Light, and the Reflection of a Sufi Tradition in the Mirror of Late Modernity&amp;rdquo;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Ali Naji Moughania&lt;/strong&gt; (Columbia University) &amp;mdash; &lt;em&gt;&amp;ldquo;Interventions in the Study of Ethics: Al-Narāqī&amp;rsquo;s Reformed Mysticism and Superintelligent AI&amp;rdquo;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
