&lt;p&gt;Panel discussion with Elizabeth Weinfield, Robinson McClellan, Susan Boynton, Anne Higonnet, and Rebecca Cypess&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Sonnambula opens their 2026&amp;ndash;2027 season with the premiere of previously lost work by &lt;a href="https://events.columbia.edu/cal/event/eventView.do?calPath=%2Fpublic%2Fcals%2FMainCal&amp;amp;guid=CAL-00bbdb7c-9f4b176f-019f-4c82d40f-00002548events@columbia.edu&amp;amp;recurrenceId="&gt;Baroque composer &amp;Eacute;lisabeth Jacquet de La Guerre at St. Paul&amp;rsquo;s Chapel on November 19&lt;/a&gt;. The program also features works by Jacquet de La Guerre&amp;rsquo;s contemporaries Marin Marais, Fran&amp;ccedil;ois Couperin, and Michel Richard Delalande, as well as a world premiere by Stephen Anthony Weinfield.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Preceding the concert, the panel discussion at the Maison Fran&amp;ccedil;aise will highlight the significance of this discovery for music and feminist history. The panel brings together Sonnambula&amp;rsquo;s Artistic Director Elizabeth Weinfield and Morgan Library Curator of Music Manuscripts and Printed Music Robinson McClellan, alongside leading scholars Susan Boynton of Columbia University, Anne Higonnet of Barnard College, and Rebecca Cypess of Yeshiva University.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This project is supported by a 2026 Chamber Music America Award, with additional support from the Lower Manhattan Cultural Council and the New York State Council on the Arts. The concert is co-sponsored by the Maison Fran&amp;ccedil;aise, Columbia College, and the Center for the Core Curriculum.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Adolescent medical decision-making can be challenging, particularly in the current climate of decreased trust in medicine. Dr. Hardart will present the case of an adolescent patient and family who refuse potentially life-saving treatment. Please join us for a conversation about how to ethically navigate such a complex case.&lt;/p&gt;
