&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Registration REQUIRED by 4pm on November 17, 2026 in order to attend this event.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Please join the East Central European Center and the Harriman Institute, with co-sponsorship from the University Seminar on Cultural Memory and the Society for Senior Scholars, for a talk by &lt;strong&gt;Barbara Kirshenblatt Gimblett&lt;/strong&gt;,&amp;nbsp;chief curator of the POLIN Museum of the History of Polish Jewry, in conversation with &lt;strong&gt;Marianne Hirsch&lt;/strong&gt;, the co-author of &lt;em&gt;Ghost of Homes:&lt;/em&gt; &lt;em&gt;The Afterlife of Czernowitz in Jewish Memory&lt;/em&gt;, about what the Jewish past means to those living in &amp;ldquo;post-Jewish&amp;rdquo; towns in Poland, Ukraine, and the rest of Central and Eastern Europe. Moderated by &lt;a href="https://harriman.columbia.edu/person/malgorzata-mazurek/"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Małgorzata Mazurek&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Kirshenblatt-Gimblett&amp;rsquo;s opening talk discusses a new category emerged: &amp;ldquo;post-Jewish&amp;rdquo; property that emerged as result of the Holocaust. As a result of the Holocaust, a new category emerged: &amp;ldquo;post-Jewish&amp;rdquo; property, which included land, buildings, and objects. Defined by dispossession resulting from the fate of Jews during and after the Holocaust, this category was the organizing concept for POLIN Museum&amp;rsquo;s recent temporary exhibition, &lt;em&gt;(post)JEWISH&amp;hellip;Shtetl Opat&amp;oacute;w Through the Eyes of Mayer Kirshenblatt&lt;/em&gt;, which explored how the shtetl became a post-Jewish town. Original memory paintings of Jewish life before the Holocaust were juxtaposed with post-Jewish objects and more broadly with the &amp;ldquo;post-Jewish&amp;rdquo; town as it evolved in the decades since the Holocaust. This project is not only about Jewish memory, but also about Polish memory &amp;ndash; what the Jewish past means to those living in these towns today. In the ensuing discussion, Hirsch and Kirshenblatt-Gimblett will explore the afterlives of violence, displacement, and dispossession in our present moment.&lt;/p&gt;

