&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Presenter:&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;Srijan Sen, MD, PhD&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Talk Title:&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;Targeting Modifiable Factors to Prevent Depression: Insights from the Intern Health Study&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;When:&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;Thursday, 11/19/2026 from 11:30 AM to 12:50 PM&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Where:&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;Hammer Health Sciences Building, Room LL-110&amp;nbsp;&lt;br /&gt;
&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;More about the speaker:&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Srijan Sen is the Frances and Kenneth Eisenberg Professor of Depression and Neurosciences and Director of the Eisenberg Family Depression Center at the University of Michigan. He received his M.D. and Ph.D. from the University of Michigan, completed a psychiatry residency at Yale University and returned to Michigan as faculty in 2009. Dr. Sen leads the Intern Health Study, a longitudinal cohort study that assesses stress and mood in training physicians, enrolling over 29,000 participants from 100+ institutions. The project has produced high-impact findings across a wide range of interdisciplinary academic topics including genomics, mobile technology, economics, gender and racial disparities, survey methodology and medical education policy. Work from the study has been published in JAMA, BMJ, New England Journal of Medicine and other leading journals and has been covered in the New York Times, Washington Post, Time Magazine and other media outlets. Dr. Sen also co-leads COMPASS, an NIH U01-funded study of over 10.000 patients on the waitlist for mental health treatment that utilizes mobile technology, genomics and health record data to match patients to eﬀective treatments and expand the capacity of the mental health care system.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Refreshments will be provided.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Join us for a conversation on the rights of older persons and the challenges of ensuring equal access to health care as populations age around the world. Drawing on her work as the former UN Independent Expert on the enjoyment of all human rights by older persons, Dr. Claudia Mahler will explore age discrimination in health systems, barriers to accessing care, and the legal protections needed to safeguard the rights and dignity of older people. She will be joined by Dr. John Beard, a leading voice on healthy ageing, who will bring a public health perspective to the conversation. Together they will examine the role of international human rights law, UN mechanisms, and legal advocacy in holding governments accountable.&lt;/p&gt;
