&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Guinea Pig Kids: AIDS Denialism and the Politics of Motherhood&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;br /&gt;
Thursday, Nov. 19, 2026 | 5:00 - 6:30 p.m. | 413 Fayerweather&lt;br /&gt;
No registration required. Only open to campus affiliates.&amp;nbsp;&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;br /&gt;
Lindsay Zafir, City College&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;br /&gt;
In the late 1990s, HIV-positive mothers across North America began refusing antiretrovirals for themselves and their children. They argued that doctors and scientists did not know better than they did how to take care of their children&amp;rsquo;s health&amp;mdash;that their innate knowledge as mothers mattered just as much as expert knowledge. This talk examines the reasons for their rebellion against state and medical authority, using it as a window into understanding the politics of the natural parenting movement from the perspective of a group of straight women who felt tainted by their association with a &amp;ldquo;gay disease.&amp;rdquo;&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;br /&gt;
Lindsay Zafir is Assistant Professor of Women&amp;#39;s, Gender, and Sexuality Studies at The City College of New York and the author of the forthcoming &lt;em&gt;State of Denial: AIDS and the Rise of the Disinformation Age&lt;/em&gt;. She is also the co-founder and co-director of the ACORN Oral History Project, which documents the history of the community organizing group ACORN.&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;br /&gt;
Sponsored by the Columbia Research Initiative on the Global History of Sexualities and the Institute for the Study of Sexuality and Gender&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Co-sponsored by the Center for the Study of Ethnicity and Race and The Society of Fellows and Heyman Center for the Humanities&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Join us for &lt;em&gt;Registering the Rift: Stoffwechsel in Marx and B&amp;uuml;chner -&amp;nbsp; &lt;/em&gt;a talk&amp;nbsp;with Johannes Wankhammer (Princeton University).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;About the event:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Ecological aesthetics has tended either to presume the holism of traditional nature&lt;br /&gt;
aesthetics or to approach disharmony and crisis through thematic representations of&lt;br /&gt;
environmental destruction. This talk prepares an alternative by examining figurations of&lt;br /&gt;
disrupted Stoffwechsel (metabolism) in Marx and B&amp;uuml;chner. Originating as a&lt;br /&gt;
physiological term for the turnover of matter necessary to sustain life, &lt;em&gt;Stoffwechsel&lt;/em&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
came to crystallize Marx&amp;rsquo;s longstanding concern with the environmental dimension of&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;social contradictions. For the mature Marx, society reproduces itself through an&lt;br /&gt;
interchange of matter with nature that is mediated by labor and disrupted by capitalist&lt;br /&gt;
production, which draws on natural cycles faster than they can be restored, opening&lt;br /&gt;
what he calls an &amp;ldquo;irreparable rift&amp;rdquo; in the social metabolism. Georg B&amp;uuml;chner&amp;rsquo;s &lt;em&gt;Woyzeck&lt;/em&gt;,&lt;br /&gt;
written in the milieu in which the term took shape, gives such disruptions a body. Paid to&lt;br /&gt;
subsist on peas for a scientific experiment, its protagonist suffers social dispossession&lt;br /&gt;
as the ruination of his metabolism. Read alongside Marx, B&amp;uuml;chner&amp;rsquo;s drama of social&lt;br /&gt;
domination becomes legible as one of creaturely and ecological depletion, in which&lt;br /&gt;
body and soil are drained by the same emerging order. These exploratory readings lay&lt;br /&gt;
the groundwork for a critical aesthetics of ecological crisis that extends a speculative&lt;br /&gt;
claim of Critical Theory&amp;mdash;that artworks manifest social contradictions in their form&amp;mdash;to&lt;br /&gt;
capital&amp;rsquo;s metabolic rifts. Rather than asking how artworks depict the nonhuman world,&lt;br /&gt;
this approach asks how they register strains in the historically specific ways societies&lt;br /&gt;
organize their metabolism with nature.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;About the Speaker:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Johannes Wankhammer, Associate Professor of German at Princeton University, works on eighteenth-century aesthetics, the cultural&lt;br /&gt;
history of attention, and the environmental humanities. He is the author of &lt;em&gt;Creatures of Attention&lt;/em&gt; (Cornell UP, 2024).&lt;/p&gt;
