&lt;p&gt;Please join the New York Chapter of New Yugoslav Studies, the Njego&amp;scaron; Endowment for Serbian Language and Culture, the East Central European Center, and the Harriman Institute for a workshop-symposium on (Post)Yugoslav Screens: Cinema, Memory, and Cultural Transformation.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Bringing together scholars of Yugoslav and post-Yugoslav cinema, media, and popular culture, this workshop-symposium explores how audiovisual practices shape, contest, and reimagine social and historical realities. Spanning socialist Yugoslavia and its post-socialist aftermath, the presentations examine how moving images both reflect and intervene in the social conditions of their production and circulation, negotiating questions of class, gender, labor, migration, and technological progress. Particular attention is given to the interplay between cultural representation, collective memory, and political struggle, and to the ways cinema and popular culture reproduce or challenge dominant historical narratives. Taken together, the workshop-symposium invites a reconsideration of the relationship between socialism and post-socialism not simply as a historical rupture, but as an ongoing process in which the meanings of the Yugoslav past&amp;mdash;and the futures once associated with it&amp;mdash;remain open to negotiation.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The symposium is free and open to everyone, including students, scholars, and the general public.&lt;/p&gt;
