&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;November Speaker: &lt;/strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;When this professor isn&amp;#39;t optimizing stellarators, she is optimizing her marathon time.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The speaker is...&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Professor Elizabeth Paul!&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;--&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Title:&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;Advances in optimization for stellarator design&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Abstract:&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;Stellarators magnetically confine plasmas without externally-driven plasma current, reducing the recirculating power and drive for instabilities in a fusion device. Modern stellarators such as Wendelstein 7-X have demonstrated the success of numerical optimization of the magnetic field for improved confinement. The immense freedom in the stellarator design space provides opportunities for reducing engineering complexity and improving performance. Despite these advances, confinement of energetic fusion products remains a critical challenge. This talk will provide an overview of recent advances in stellarator optimization and their application to the design of new experiments in the Columbia Plasma Laboratory. I will also discuss reduced models for nonlinear energetic-particle dynamics, including trapped-particle resonances and interactions with Alfv&amp;eacute;n eigenmodes, and their application to assessing confinement in stellarator reactor concepts in collaboration with industry partners.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;--&lt;/p&gt;
