&lt;p&gt;How do clothes make a man? Margaret Waller&amp;rsquo;s talk will draw on her book, &lt;em&gt;Napoleon&amp;rsquo;s Closet,&lt;/em&gt; which explores the paradoxes of modern male power as a matter of attire in the lives of two &amp;ldquo;emperors&amp;quot; before and after the French Revolution. Napoleon Bonaparte, the Corsican upstart, crowned himself emperor of the French. His equally unlikely contemporary, Pierre Joseph Antoine Leboux La M&amp;eacute;sang&amp;egrave;re, a defrocked priest, invented the &amp;ldquo;women&amp;rsquo;s&amp;rdquo; fashion press and made it his empire. After the French Revolution, the two men helped redefine male power dressing as a matter of dressing up, dressing down, and closeting. Together, their stories illuminate how uniformity, misogyny, and homophobia shaped recognizably modern notions of normative masculinity, queerness, and &amp;ldquo;the closet.&amp;rdquo;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Margaret Waller&lt;/strong&gt; is Professor Emerita of French at Pomona College and author of &lt;em&gt;The Male Malady: Fictions of Impotence in the French Romantic Nove&lt;/em&gt;l. Her newest book is &lt;em&gt;Napoleon&amp;rsquo;s Closet: The Emperor, the Priest, and the Men Who Invented Modern Fashion&lt;/em&gt; (Columbia University Press).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Anne Higonnet&lt;/strong&gt; is Barbara Novak Professor of Art History at Barnard College.&amp;nbsp;Her most recent books include &lt;em&gt;Liberty, Equality, Fashion; the Women Who Styled the French Revolution &lt;/em&gt;(Norton &amp;amp; Co., 2024); &lt;em&gt;A Museum of One&amp;#39;s Own; Private Collecting, Public Gift &lt;/em&gt;(Periscope Publishing, 2009); and &lt;em&gt;Lewis Carroll&lt;/em&gt; (Phaidon, 2008). Most recently, she directed a website about the world&amp;rsquo;s rarest and most radical fashion plates: &lt;a href="https://stylerevolution.github.io/"&gt;https://stylerevolution.github.io/&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

