&lt;p&gt;Join us for a discussion celebrating the release of Research: A Manifesto by &lt;strong&gt;Peter N. Miller&lt;/strong&gt;, PhD. From medical breakthroughs and technological advances to our understanding of human history and the natural world, research shapes nearly every aspect of modern life. But how did research become such a powerful force, and what makes it possible? In his new book, Miller traces the history of research, explores its relationship with universities and government, and considers what is at stake for the future of discovery.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This conversation will be led by &lt;strong&gt;Daphna Shohamy&lt;/strong&gt;, PhD, Kavli Professor of Brain Science, Director of Columbia&amp;rsquo;s Zuckerman Mind Brain Behavior Institute, and Co-Director of Columbia&amp;rsquo;s Kavli Institute for Brain Science.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This event is open to the entire Columbia community.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Monthly Seminar in December, hosted by the Center for Sexual and Gender Minority Health Research at Columbia University School of Nursing, will feature &lt;strong&gt;Harry Barbee, PhD&lt;/strong&gt;, Assistant Professor in the Department of Health, Behavior and Society at the Johns Hopkins Bloomberg School of Public Health. The title of their talk is &amp;ldquo;&lt;em&gt;What Gets Under the Skin: Life-Course Minority Stress and Cognitive Aging Among Sexual and Gender Minority Adults&lt;/em&gt;.&amp;rdquo;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Dr. Barbee&amp;rsquo;s research and teaching focus on aging, health, and policy, particularly the social factors that influence LGBTQ+ people&amp;rsquo;s health and aging. They also serve as Co-Lead of Research and Mentorship at the Center for Transgender and Gender Expansive Health.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This event is open to everyone and will be offered in a hybrid format! Attendees may join virtually or in person; however, we ask those planning to attend in person to RSVP in advance for logistical purposes. Virtual attendees may join via:&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
